<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the Archspace app and its helpers
  (ARCHITECTURE §13 / ADR-0012).

  The rule for this file: an entitlement is a hole in the hardened runtime, so
  each one below has to name the concrete thing that breaks without it. Nothing
  is here "because Electron templates have it".

  NOTE ON WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT:

  * No App Sandbox key (`com.apple.security.app-sandbox`). The sandbox forbids
    spawning arbitrary child processes; Archspace's spine is spawning stdio MCP
    servers (§9.2) and one child process per installed plugin (§8.1). Turning
    the sandbox on would delete the extension model, which is why there is no
    Mac App Store build. Because the sandbox is off, the sandbox-only keys
    (`com.apple.security.inherit`, `files.user-selected.read-write`,
    `network.client`, `device.*`) are meaningless here and are not listed —
    outside the sandbox the app already has the user's own file and network
    access, mediated by TCC prompts.

  * No `com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory`. Modern Electron
    needs `allow-jit` only. If a future Electron or a native plugin dependency
    turns out to need it, add it with a note saying what failed — do not add it
    pre-emptively.

  * No `com.apple.security.cs.debugger`, no
    `com.apple.security.automation.apple-events`. The app scripts nothing and
    debugs nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime. Without this, the
    renderer and every Node-side process crash on launch under the hardened
    runtime. This is the one non-negotiable Electron entitlement.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Plugins are user-installed code and may ship native `.node` addons (the
    consent flow surfaces "this plugin contains native code" for exactly this
    reason — ADR-0012 §6). Library validation would refuse to load any dynamic
    library not signed by our Team ID, so a plugin with a native dependency
    could never load. This is the entitlement that makes the plugin process
    model work, and it is the largest hole in this file: it is the reason
    ADR-0008's honesty clause says v1's plugin boundary is fault isolation and
    permission mediation, NOT a hardened security sandbox.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Plugin child processes are launched as `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE` forks of our
    own signed binary (see PluginSpawn in @archspace/plugin-host), and stdio MCP
    servers are spawned with a caller-supplied `env` block (McpStdioBinding.env).
    The hardened runtime restricts environment variables inherited across a
    signed binary's exec; this entitlement is the documented escape hatch.

    HONEST STATUS: this has NOT been verified against a real signed build — no
    Developer ID identity exists in this environment (see docs/releasing.md).
    It is here because removing an entitlement after a green signed build is
    cheap and safe, whereas debugging a silently-dead plugin process on a
    notarized build is not. On the first real signed run, try removing it: if
    the plugin-host crash-containment test and an stdio MCP server both still
    work in the packaged app, delete this key and this comment.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
